 {"id": "OBSDB-007", "title": "[P1] Deploy the /metrics endpoint: restart the live daemon, wire the scrape, add the alert rules that would have caught OBSDB-006's false silence", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-03T23:58:56Z", "reasoning": "OBSDB-001 is landed on main (a530621c + 800fc4a6) and proven on a scratch daemon, but the production daemon at :3000 still answers /metrics with 401 because it is running code from before the merge (PID 3856050, started 2026-11-03 17:06:06). A metric nobody scrapes is the same lie as an alert file nobody reads: the hardening skill names that pattern explicitly. Until a scrape exists, an outage still gets discovered by a human noticing. Two further facts make this urgent: (a) the merge added a real dependency (prom-client), so a restart of the daemon without 'pnpm install' in that checkout would crash it - that install is now done, but the restart is owner-gated; (b) there are no alert rules anywhere for duckbrain, so even after a restart nothing consumes the series.", "acceptance_criteria": "The live daemon serves /metrics and something other than a human reads it. Steps, each with pasted evidence: (1) after the owner authorises a restart, 'curl -s http://127.0.0.1:3000/metrics | head -1' returns '# HELP duckbrain_http_up' with no credentials, and /health is still 200 healthy; (2) a scrape job exists in the monitoring stack (fleet-monitor scrape config, or a systemd timer that writes a snapshot) with a proven successful scrape - show the scraped sample count and the timestamp; (3) alert rules exist for the three conditions this row's audit named as invisible: duckbrain_http_up == 0 (down), duckbrain_embedding_provider_healthy == 0 (degraded provider), and a rising ratio of duckbrain_reads_total{status_class=~\"4xx|5xx\"} - each rule must be FIRED once artificially (e.g. stop a scratch daemon, or point the rule at a fixture) and shown to reach its notification target, not merely be defined; (4) confirm liveness alerts use duckbrain_http_up (a gauge, always present) and NOT absent(duckbrain_reads_total) - the labeled counter legitimately has no series until its first read. Owner gate: the restart.", "updated_at": "2026-11-03T23:58:56Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "a859ac9021546216502d1aff213202ea2a7e278a338df3b669e7a14a09ba03a1"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null}
-{"id": "HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003", "title": "[P1] Hermes memory provider went 'unavailable' because /health could be rate-limited — 127.0.0.1 is one shared token bucket for every local caller", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T00:16:56Z", "reasoning": "Bane reported 2026-11-03: 'Memory provider duckbrain is selected but reports unavailable — external memory is disabled for this session'. Root cause measured, not guessed: (1) the daemon on :3000 runs --rate-limit 600 as a PER-IP token bucket (src/auth/ratelimit.ts), and every caller on this host — fleet agents, cron, MCP servers, supervisors, probes — shares 127.0.0.1; (2) /health was registered AFTER rateLimitMiddleware (src/cli/http.ts:688 vs :564), so it was itself rate-limited; (3) ten sequential unauthenticated probes returned 8x 200 (2.6ms) and 2x 429 (0.6ms) while the process was healthy; (4) the provider's is_available() required exactly r.status == 200, so one throttled probe flipped the whole session to 'external memory disabled'. /metrics escaped this only because OBSDB-001 mounted it above the limiter. The systemic half is not fixed by the route move: a single burst from ONE agent can still throttle every other local caller's API traffic, which is why this row stays open after the liveness fix.", "acceptance_criteria": "Two halves. (A) LIVENESS — landed 121aa62c: /health is registered with /metrics above the limiter; a new suite proves 25 /health probes and 25 /metrics scrapes at rateLimit=2 yield zero 429s while /stats still 429s with Retry-After (and the test fails with the fix stashed). The provider (~/.hermes/plugins/duckbrain) now treats 429/503 from a live socket as ALIVE and only 401/403/no-token/refused as unavailable, proven against local servers for 200/429/503/401/403/refused. Verify live AFTER the daemon restart: 25 back-to-back curl /health all answer 200 or 503, never 429. (B) SYSTEMIC (still open) — local callers must not share one throttle bucket in a way that lets one agent starve the rest: either exempt loopback/liveness-class routes explicitly with a documented list, or split the bucket (per-principal or per-socket class), or raise the local budget with a measured basis. Prove with a transcript: drive 200 rapid requests from one local caller and show another local caller's /api/memories still answers 200 (i.e. one caller cannot starve another). Do not simply raise the limit without the second-caller proof.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T00:54:05Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "d148d11072b6a4c3934b30d571fa060287252946c4483fce9496666be0848851"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "LIVENESS HALF DONE 121aa62c, but it went RED on CI (run 41745657088, 3 failures) and the repair is 37f81e19: (a) tests/http-ratelimit.int.test.ts hammered /health as the cheapest URL, so an exempt /health produced no 429 and the X-RateLimit-Limit assertion read a 503 — they now hammer /api/namespaces, treating 401 as 'under the limit' because the limiter runs before auth; (b) tests/ratelimit-squatter.int.test.ts had depended on draining A's /health bucket so the readiness poll saw 429 from the squatter — with /health exempt, A answers 200 instantly and waitForUrl correctly read a 200 from a daemon it never started, so the test now waits for B to actually lose the bind race before asking the guard (same shape, made explicit instead of accidental). Both green locally under vitest.integration.config.ts (6 tests). PROCESS ERROR WORTH RECORDING: I ran targeted suites and reported success, and CI caught what they missed — twice (the second: OBSDB-002 grew the family set to eight and OBSDB-001's exact-shape test went red, fixed in 9a4aeb57). CI is GREEN on 9a4aeb57. Local full-suite runs still show ~10 contention failures in ddl-* and memories-search suites that all pass in isolation — the known load flakes, NOT fixed here. (B) SYSTEMIC still open: one local caller can still starve another's API traffic.\n\nMEASURED BASIS FOR THE LIMIT RAISE (Bane: we will raise it soon). Ledger census over 942,228 rows, 2026-10-07T12:10Z..2026-11-04T00:53Z: 429s RECORDED IN THE LEDGER = 0, and that zero is the blind spot itself — the limiter runs before the read ledger, so a refusal cannot be recorded there. It is not evidence of no refusals: ten sequential /health probes on the live daemon produced 2x429 while the ledger shows none. Sustained rate: 7d peak 759/min, p99 532/min, p50 3/min, 56 of 7,362 active minutes (0.76%) above the 600 cap, ZERO minutes above 1000; 24h peak 693/min, p99 566/min, 9 minutes (0.89%) above 600. Bucket shape (src/auth/ratelimit.ts:90): maxTokens = burstSize ?? requestsPerMinute = 600, refill 600/60000 = 0.01 tokens/ms = 10 req/s — so sustained demand above 10/s drains the bucket and the LAST callers of that minute get 429 regardless of their own rate. That matches the observed peak (693/min ~ 11.5/s for a full minute). RECOMMENDATION with a basis: requestsPerMinute 600 -> 1200 (never exceeded in 7d, 2x headroom) and set burstSize explicitly to 300 (so a single agent's burst cannot empty a bucket sized for the whole fleet); then re-measure rejected_total{reason=rate_limit} for 48h and only raise again if it is still non-zero. Do NOT raise without OBSDB-002 live: before it, a raise is unverifiable because refusals were invisible."}
