 {"id": "HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-004", "title": "[P2] Two lockfiles must be updated in lockstep and nothing reminds you — adding prom-client broke CI twice today", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T01:05:42Z", "reasoning": "The repo tracks BOTH pnpm-lock.yaml (CI installs with 'pnpm install --frozen-lockfile', .github/workflows/ci.yml:35) and package-lock.json (the Dockerfile installs with 'npm ci', Dockerfile:8). Adding prom-client in OBSDB-001 updated only pnpm, so src/test/lockfile-sync.test.ts failed the first full suite and then the CI run; the repair (90e20a0e) regenerated package-lock.json with 'npm install --package-lock-only --ignore-scripts' (+39 lines). The same class of drift is one dependency away from happening again and the reminder is a test that fails 4 minutes into a suite rather than a step that cannot be skipped. Not urgent, not cosmetic: a red main blocks every worker behind it.", "acceptance_criteria": "Make the drift impossible or immediately loud, prove it, and do not remove the npm lockfile without an owner decision (the Dockerfile depends on it). Options, pick and prove ONE: (A) a pre-commit/CI step that runs both 'pnpm install --lockfile-only' and 'npm install --package-lock-only --ignore-scripts' whenever package.json changes, and fails with a diff if either lockfile is dirty — prove by adding a throwaway dependency and showing the gate fires BEFORE the test suite; (B) a single source of truth (drop one lockfile and the install path that needs it), which requires an owner decision and a Dockerfile change — prove with a green docker build leg. Either way: record the chosen rule in the contributing docs, and show 'pnpm test:run' plus the docker leg green on the change.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T01:05:42Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "0b50a4ab40a0a11c059be5c38b333dd12eb0b6cc9960667b431901e21d2bb71a"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_su