 {"id": "PERF-DUCKBRAIN-001", "title": "[P1] 11% of /api/memories reads take >=3s and the cause is FILE COUNT (20k-29k jsonl files per namespace), not embeddings", "status": "blocked", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T01:10:15Z", "reasoning": "Measured against the live daemon 2026-11-04 (read-only, authenticated). Ledger census over 52,139 GET /api/memories calls: 55.7% under 300ms (median 73ms), 20.1% in 1-3s, 8.6% in 3-10s, 2.9% over 10s -> 11.45% at >=3s, and 5,968 of those slow calls carried NO query string, so this is the plain LIST path, not semantic search. My first hypothesis (embedding in the path) was wrong. Direct probes isolate the real variable: namespace 'hermes' (6 jsonl files, 145 rows, 5.6M) answers limit=10 in 32ms and limit=200 in 33ms; 'coding-hermes' (20,054 files, 494,973 rows, 2.3G) answers limit=10 in 1.42-2.00s across three repeats; 'fleet-quality' (29,080 files, 94,483 rows, 346M) took 13.26s cold then 1.82s warm. Latency tracks FILE COUNT, not row count and not payload (fleet-quality returns 2.05MB for limit=200 yet beats a 1.4s limit=10 on file-dense coding-hermes). Two consequences: the tail is a per-read directory-walk/metadata cost that no cache can hide on the first call, and 'limit' buys nothing because selection still has to visit the files.", "acceptance_criteria": "Cut the tail with a mechanism, not a cache. (1) Reproduce: a script that, for each of coding-hermes and fleet-quality, runs limit=10 three times cold and reports median vs the file count — committed so the numbers are re-derivable, not quoted. (2) Fix the read path so a limit=10 list does not depend on total file count: compact/merge the namespace's small jsonl files (there is already squash/compaction machinery — prove which one applies) and/or add a bounded index (e.g. newest-N by mtime/segment ordering) that the list endpoint consults before opening files. (3) Prove the effect with the same script: cold limit=10 on both namespaces under 500ms, and no row lost — compare namespaces prune --dry-run plus per-namespace row counts before/after, and confirm GET /api/memories?namespace=X&limit=200 returns the same first-200 newest rows as before the change. (4) Show the ledger's own p95 for GET /api/memories falling after the fix (or state honestly that the window is too short to move p95 yet and give a sampled measurement instead). Do NOT simply raise caches or add a bigger timeout.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T16:13:43Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "662b29f55d17e638d0a20330321b51bc441043ac924a892cf6e2b22b6c7010ab"}, "worker_status": "done", "commit_hash": "e623f99e76c54075634a4def3daa1deb1be7f15f", "guard_result": "PASS", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "gitreins tier1 PASS, tier2 INCOMPLETE on 3 of 5 outcomes. Landed e623f99e: bounded top-k + arg_max total for the plain list path, filtered paths verbatim, 630 tests pass, limit=200 byte-identical (total 241,901 / 53,937).", "foreman_note": "PROOF, PART 1 (compaction) — done on the COPY /home/kara/.duckbrain-live/namespaces, production untouched. 'duckbrain squash --segments --partition=config/2026-08 --namespace=coding-hermes' took the partition 10,000 segments -> 22 (dry-run) and 10,001 files -> 23 on disk; event/2026-08 went 9,798 -> 44. RECORDS PRESERVED: production config/2026-08 = 113,311 rows across 10,001 files; compacted copy = 113,311 rows across 23 files (ROWS MATCH). LATENCY, same machine, same daemon code, --auth=none so no token can distort the read (script /home/kara/clock/prove-perf001-compaction.sh, port 28701): production (fragmented) limit=10 was 1.42/1.88/2.00s across three runs; compacted copy limit=10 is 0.98s cold then 0.47/0.50s warm, and limit=200 returns 1.04MB in 0.51s. So file-count compaction is worth ~3-4x and is the cheap half. IT IS NOT THE WHOLE FIX: the compacted tree still holds ~300 files across partitions (event/2026-09 = 231, plus 23 + 44 + audit) and a limit=10 read still pays ~0.47s, which is consistent with the