mespace — the one behind Bane's own assistant memory — so the tail is not only a fleet-throughput issue. scheduler at 258,578 files is 67% of the entire substrate by file count and was not in the original measurement at all. Consequence for the acceptance criteria: scale the proof per namespace (rows preserved, latency before/after) rather than declaring victory on coding-hermes alone, and expect the scheduler namespace to dominate the wall-clock of any compaction run.\n\nWorker: deepseek-v4-pro (attempts 1-2 on gpt-6-luna returned partial/design-only; attempt 3 landed). Bench: scripts/perf/perf001-list-latency.mjs (cold+warm labelled). Verdict: .gitreins/history/2026-11-04/e5650949/verdict.json. First judge run 61c5252d tier1 flaked on src/git/autocommit-supapair.test.ts under load; the same test passes in isolation (3/3) and the rerun's tier1 is PASS.", "blocked_reason": "CODE HALF LANDED, DATA HALF OWNER-GATED: the O(nRows log nRows) dedup sort is gone from the plain list path (cold coding-hermes 508->275ms, warm 355->230ms on the compacted copy), but limit=10 still scales with FILE COUNT because collectJsonlFiles() enumerates every segment (fleet-quality 29,080 files = 5.3s cold / 1.7s warm). The remaining half needs the proven segment compaction run against PRODUCTION namespaces, which is a live-data operation plus a daemon restart — Bane's call per HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-002. Filed as PERF-DUCKBRAIN-002.", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/duckbrain-PERF-DUCKBRAIN-001", "branch": "wt/PERF-DUCKBRAIN-001"}
 {"id": "SUPA9-PK-FILTER-001", "title": "[P0] GET ignores ?pk=eq.N while PATCH/DELETE require it — the same address means 'one row' to a write verb and 'all rows' to a read", "status": "complete", "priority": "P0", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T02:30:55Z", "reasoning": "Found by driving the declared-table REST surface end to end on a scratch daemon 2026-11-04 (/home/kara/clock/prove-supa-surface.sh, port 28801/28802, table 'widgets' with primary key id). PATCH and DELETE address rows with ?pk=eq.<value> and both work ({\"updated\":1}, {\"deleted\":1}); a GET with the SAME ?pk=eq.1 returns EVERY row in the table. It is not a general 'unknown filter is ignored' policy either: ?bogus=eq.9 is correctly refused with 400 VALIDATION_ERROR naming the declared columns, and ?id=eq.1 correctly filters on the real column. So 'pk' is special-cased into an accepted-but-unapplied parameter on the read path. Concretely: with 3 rows present, GET ?pk=eq.1 -> all 3 rows, GET ?id=eq.1 -> the one row. Why it matters: the API teaches callers that pk addresses a row (it does for PATCH/DELETE), so an agent that patches and then verifies with the same URL is told its change either did nothing or hit everything — and a delete verification reads as 'still there' only because the row is genuinely gone. That is the exact class of inconsistency that gets an autonomous caller to retry a write that already succeeded.", "acceptance_criteria": "Make read addressing agree with write addressing, and prove all three cases. (1) GET /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table?pk=eq.<v> returns exactly the row whose declared primary key equals v (or a documented 400 if the table declares no primary — not a silent full scan). (2) A falsifier in tests: with 3 rows, GET ?pk=eq.<one> returns 1 row while GET with no filter returns 3, and GET ?<pkcolumn>=eq.<one> returns the same 1 row (alias must not disagree with the column form). (3) An unknown filter column still refuses with VALIDATION_ERROR naming the declared columns (do not relax that to make the alias work). (4) PATCH and DELETE continue to accept ?pk=eq. and their responses are unchanged — prove with the existing suites plus one end-to-end run of declare -> insert 3 -> GET by pk -> PATCH by pk -> DELETE by pk -> GET by pk returns 0. Update the docs/spec (SUPA-3) to state the addressing rule once, so read and write verbs cannot drift again.", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T13:52:07Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "cb86d95abac1f12b5de7354a2c1d471a4b402b524a98fe14632956511d1322e9", "evidence": [{"run_id":"1e915eb9","ts":"2026-11-04T14:34:15Z","tree_fp":"c6f70146d92398ba31db76c787adef85c35955e549bf7834ea21be4a66890633","artifacts":[".gitreins/history/2026-11-04/1e915eb9/verdict.json"]}]}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": "1c8e4be9", "guard_result": "SKIP", "ci_result": "GREEN", "worker_summary": "GET now honours the pk=eq primary-key alias: shared resolvePkFilter() (tables.ts) used by both write verbs and filterParams() on the read path; non-eq pk 400s, no-pk tables 400 instead of returning every row. impl a6df3394, merge 1c8e4be9. Verified: 6 new regression tests (RED pre-fix 4 failed/2 passed, GREEN at HEAD 6/6), focused 52/52, tsc clean, worker full suite 252 files/2094 tests green, test-counts.json resynced in-commit.", "foreman_note": "PRIORITY RAISED TO P0 by Bane 2026-11-04. Rationale: it is the only KNOWN live inconsistency in the Supabase-shaped REST surface, and it corrupts the thing DuckBrain sells — an agent building a feature directly on the database cannot trust that a read address means the same rows as a write address. Fix before the next release cut and before any comparison work is published."}
diff --git a/.gitreins/history/2026-11-05/680dd883/commit.patch b/.gitreins/history/2026-11-05/680dd883/commit.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..845f44d9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.gitreins/history/2026-11-05/680dd883/commit.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,748 @@
+commit 91d92c515600d7fdace216ebe00409a99964943c
+Author:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
+AuthorDate: Thu Nov 5 11:46:53 2026 -0500
+Commit:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
+CommitDate: Thu Nov 5 11:46:53 2026 -0500
+
+    fix(http): key the rate-limit bucket on the principal, not the peer IP (HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003 B)
+    
+    Every fleet caller on this host shares 127.0.0.1, so the per-IP token bucket
+    collapsed them into one budget: one agent's burst returned 429 to every other
+    agent's requests. The limiter now spends from `principal:<sha256(key)>` when the
+    live credential snapshot knows the presented x-api-key (constant-time match,
+    cached snapshot), and from `ip:<peer>` for anonymous / unrecognized / basic-auth
+    callers — so guessing a key mints no fresh budget and credential stuffing stays
+    bounded by address. Store size is capped with oldest-bucket eviction.
+    
+    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
+---
+ src/auth/ratelimit.test.ts            | 114 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
+ src/auth/ratelimit.ts                 | 109 +++++++++++++++++++++++++--
+ src/auth/storeSchema.ts               |  49 ++++++++++++
+ src/auth/token-lifecycle.test.ts      |  54 +++++++++++++-
+ src/cli/http.ts                       |  87 +++++++++++++++++----
+ test-counts.json                      |   4 +-
+ tests/ratelimit-principal.int.test.ts | 137 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
+ 7 files changed, 530 insertions(+), 24 deletions(-)
+
+diff --git a/src/auth/ratelimit.test.ts b/src/auth/ratelimit.test.ts
+index f7056e36..71b53dc8 100644
+--- a/src/auth/ratelimit.test.ts
++++ b/src/auth/ratelimit.test.ts
+@@ -286,3 +286,117 @@ describe("rateLimitMiddleware 429 observability", () => {
+     }
+   });
+ });
++
++/**
++ * HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003 (B): the bucket must be the PRINCIPAL, not the peer IP.
++ *
++ * The live failure this pins: every fleet caller on a host shares 127.0.0.1, so
++ * one agent's burst drained the single per-IP bucket and every other agent's
++ * request came back 429 while the daemon was healthy. These tests fail on the
++ * pre-fix middleware, where the second caller below is refused.
++ */
++describe("HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003 principal bucketing", () => {
++  const principalKey = (req: Partial<Request>, ip: string): string =>
++    `principal:${String(req.headers?.["x-api-key"] ?? ip)}`;
++
++  it("throttles each principal separately, so one caller cannot starve another on the same IP", () => {
++    const middleware = rateLimitMiddleware({
++      requestsPerMinute: 1,
++      bucketKeyOf: principalKey,
++    });
++
++    // Caller A burns its only token.
++    const a1 = mockNext();
++    middleware(
++      mockReq({ ip: "127.0.0.1", headers: { "x-api-key": "harden003-a" } }) as Request,
++      mockRes().res as Response,
++      a1,
++    );
++    expect(a1).toHaveBeenCalled();
++
++    // Caller A's second request is refused — the limit is real, not disabled.
++    const aRefusal = mockRes();
++    middleware(
++      mockReq({ ip: "127.0.0.1", headers: { "x-api-key": "harden003-a" } }) as Request,
++      aRefusal.res as Response,
++      mockNext(),
++    );
++    expect(aRefusal.status).toHaveBeenCalledWith(429);
++
++    // Caller B, SAME peer IP, has its own budget and is served.
++    const bNext = mockNext();
++    const { status: bStatus } = mockRes();
++    middleware(
++      mockReq({ ip: "127.0.0.1", headers: { "x-api-key": "harden003-b" } }) as Request,
++      mockRes().res as Response,
++      bNext,
++    );
++    expect(bNext).toHaveBeenCalled();
++    expect(bStatus).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
++  });
++
++  it("refuses the same principal twice at rate 1 (control: the bucket still empties)", () => {
++    const middleware = rateLimitMiddleware({
++      requestsPerMinute: 1,
++      bucketKeyOf: principalKey,
++    });
++    const req = mockReq({
++      ip: "127.0.0.1",
++      headers: { "x-api-key": "harden003-control" },
++    }) as Request;
++
++    middleware(req, mockRes().res as Response, mockNext());
++    const { res, status } = mockRes();
++    middleware(req, res as Response, mockNext());
++    expect(status).toHaveBeenCalledWith(429);
++  });
++
++  it("falls back to the peer-IP bucket when the resolver throws, without failing the request", () => {
++    const warn = vi.spyOn(console, "warn").mockImplementation(() => {});
++    try {
++      const middleware = rateLimitMiddleware({
++        requestsPerMinute: 1,
++        bucketKeyOf: () => {
++          throw new Error("resolver exploded");
++        },
++      });
++      const output = mockRes();
++      const next = mockNext();
++      middleware(
++        mockReq({ ip: "10.9.9.9" }) as Request,
++        output.res as Response,
++        next,
++      );
++      expect(next).toHaveBeenCalled();
++      expect(warn).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
++      expect(String(warn.mock.calls[0][0])).toContain(
++        "rate_limit_bucket_resolver_failed",
++      );
++      // ... and the fallback bucket is still a working bucket.
++      const { status } = mockRes();
++      middleware(
++        mockReq({ ip: "10.9.9.9" }) as Request,
++        mockRes().res as Response,
++        mockNext(),
++      );
++      expect(status).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
++    } finally {
++      warn.mockRestore();
++    }
++  });
++
++  it("bounds the bucket store: a new principal beyond maxBuckets evicts instead of growing forever", () => {
++    const middleware = rateLimitMiddleware({
++      requestsPerMinute: 1,
++      maxBuckets: 2,
++      bucketKeyOf: (_req, ip) => `ip:${ip}`,
++    });
++    for (const ip of ["10.7.0.1", "10.7.0.2", "10.7.0.3"]) {
++      const next = mockNext();
++      middleware(mockReq({ ip }) as Request, mockRes().res as Response, next);
++      // Every caller is admitted: the store made room by evicting the oldest
++      // bucket instead of refusing (or growing without bound).
++      expect(next).toHaveBeenCalled();
++    }
++  });
++});
+diff --git a/src/auth/ratelimit.ts b/src/auth/ratelimit.ts
+index f367d778..2409ca5e 100644
+--- a/src/auth/ratelimit.ts
++++ b/src/auth/ratelimit.ts
+@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
+  * Configurable via RateLimitConfig.
+  */
+ 
++import { createHash } from "node:crypto";
+ import { Request, Response, NextFunction, RequestHandler } from "express";
+ 
+ /**
+@@ -18,6 +19,32 @@ export interface RateLimitConfig {
+   requestsPerMinute: number;
+   /** Burst size: allows temporary spikes above the rate (default: same as requestsPerMinute) */
+   burstSize?: number;
++  /**
++   * HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003 (B): the identity a request is throttled against.
++   *
++   * The bucket used to be keyed on the peer IP alone. On a host where every
++   * caller is local — agents, cron, MCP servers, supervisors — that is ONE
++   * bucket, so one agent's burst throttled every other agent on the machine
++   * (/health was the first casualty, then the Hermes memory provider). The
++   * resolver lets the server key on the PRINCIPAL (a credential the auth layer
++   * already knows) while leaving anonymous and unrecognized callers on the
++   * peer-IP bucket, so credential stuffing and anonymous floods stay bounded.
++   *
++   * Default: the peer IP — the historical per-IP behavior.
++   */
++  bucketKeyOf?: (req: Request, ip: string) => string;
++  /**
++   * HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003 (B): upper bound on simultaneously tracked buckets.
++   *
++   * A principal bucket key is derived from a caller-supplied credential, so an
++   * uncapped store would let a client mint buckets without limit and turn the
++   * limiter into a memory leak. At the cap the oldest buckets are evicted
++   * first: they are the ones closest to fully refilled, so dropping them costs
++   * the least accuracy. Eviction does reset an evicted caller's budget, which
++   * is the price of a bounded store — the default is far above any plausible
++   * live principal count.
++   */
++  maxBuckets?: number;
+   /**
+    * OBSDB-002: sink for each REFUSED request (the 429 below).
+    *
+@@ -44,18 +71,44 @@ interface RateLimitEntry {
+ 
+ /**
+  * In-memory rate limit store
+- * Keyed by IP address
++ *
++ * Keyed by BUCKET KEY: `ip:<peer>` for anonymous / unrecognized callers, or
++ * `principal:<digest>` for a caller whose credential the server recognizes
++ * (HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003 (B)). Before that change the key was always the peer
++ * IP, which collapsed every local caller into one bucket.
+  */
+ interface RateLimitStore {
+-  [ip: string]: RateLimitEntry;
++  [bucket: string]: RateLimitEntry;
+ }
+ 
+ // Global store — shared across all middleware instances
+ const store: RateLimitStore = {};
+ 
++/**
++ * HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003 (B): bucket key for a caller we cannot name — the peer
++ * address, exactly the pre-existing per-IP behavior.
++ */
++export function ipBucketKey(ip: string): string {
++  return `ip:${ip}`;
++}
++
++/**
++ * HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003 (B): bucket key for a named principal.
++ *
++ * The credential is hashed, never stored: the limiter keeps no secret material,
++ * and rotating a token rotates its bucket for free. Truncated to 128 bits —
++ * enough that two live credentials cannot collide.
++ */
++export function principalBucketKey(credential: string): string {
++  const digest = createHash("sha256").update(credential, "utf-8").digest("hex");
++  return `principal:${digest.slice(0, 32)}`;
++}
++
+ // Cleanup interval (every 5 minutes, remove entries older than 10 minutes)
+ const CLEANUP_INTERVAL_MS = 5 * 60 * 1000;
+ const ENTRY_MAX_AGE_MS = 10 * 60 * 1000;
++// HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-003 (B): bound on live buckets (see RateLimitConfig.maxBuckets).
++const DEFAULT_MAX_BUCKETS = 20_000;
+ let lastCleanup = Date.now();
+ 
+ /**
+@@ -75,6 +128,30 @@ function cleanupStore(): void {
